  "response": "Attribution of acute liver injury to a newly started medication rests on a negative search rather than on any confirmatory test, because drug-induced liver injury is a diagnosis of exclusion. It is for precisely that reason that serological testing for hepatitis A, B, C, and E is performed to exclude viral causes of acute liver injury. In the case at hand the hepatitis E RNA assay admits of only two outcomes, and exactly one of them holds, not both: either the patient's hepatitis E RNA test was negative, or the patient's hepatitis E RNA test was positive. That same exclusionary requirement is what prompts clinicians to measure antinuclear antibody, anti-smooth muscle antibody, and serum IgG to evaluate for autoimmune hepatitis.\n\nOnce competing causes have been ranked, adjudication turns on the biochemical signature of the injury itself. Because the R value is calculated as the ALT level divided by its upper limit of normal over the alkaline phosphatase level divided by its upper limit of normal, the result is a scale on which an R value of 5 or greater indicates a hepatocellular pattern of liver injury. Pattern assignment then narrows the field of plausible agents, since individual hepatotoxins carry characteristic signatures on liver biochemistry and on histology. Since a ratio at or above five fixes the injury at the hepatocellular pole, it follows that acetaminophen hepatotoxicity characteristically produces a cholestatic pattern with periportal necrosis.\n\nSeverity grading follows pattern assignment and carries the greatest prognostic weight in the adjudication. Hy's law describes ALT greater than three times the upper limit of normal accompanied by total bilirubin greater than twice the upper limit of normal without significant alkaline phosphatase elevation. The rule is invoked by name in regulatory review. Hy's law requires a normal serum total bilirubin at the time of aminotransferase elevation. In practice, a case satisfying that combination of thresholds prompts immediate withdrawal of the suspect agent and close biochemical observation.\n\nChronology does much of the remaining work. Biochemical improvement after withdrawal of the suspect drug is termed dechallenge and supports drug causality; only afterwards, and in current practice almost never by deliberate design, does the question of reexposure arise, recurrence of liver injury upon reexposure to the same drug being regarded as strong evidence of drug causation. Taken together, the fall in aminotransferases off the agent and their prompt return on renewed exposure show that the newly started medication was hepatotoxic in this patient. Drug hepatotoxicity and contributory liver disease are not competing verdicts here: at least one of the two accounts\u2014the hepatotoxicity of the newly started medication in this patient, or the contribution of an underlying liver disease process to the acute liver injury\u2014must be operative, and both may well be. Expert panels therefore record a probability grade rather than a verdict, and that grade is revised as the biochemical course unfolds.",
